Purpose: To explain when firms converge toward or diverge from industry norms in turbulent environments by introducing a normative alignment framework grounded in Ansoff's Strategic Success Hypothesis (SSH). While prior benchmarking research models how firms move relative to industry averages, it offers limited guidance on when such movements are strategically appropriate.&nbsp;
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach: Using a longitudinal panel of NASDAQ-listed technology firms (2006-2015), we code strategic aggressiveness (SA) and capability responsiveness (CR) from auditable disclosures and assess environmental turbulence (ETL) at the firm-year level. Strategic alignment is decomposed into an external calibration gap (|SA-ETL|) and an internal supportability gap (|CR-SA|). Benchmark deviation-defined as the focal-firm-excluded industry-year difference in X1-is modeled in first differences (Δ|Distance|) using firm and year fixed-effects regressions with lagged ETL and gap interactions. Findings: Environmental turbulence is associated with divergence among relatively aligned firms (low misalignment) and convergence among misaligned firms (high misalignment), consistent with SSH's alignment logic. Industry-level turbulence specifications yield statistically significant moderation effects. Supplemental analyses indicate that benchmark shifts amplify convergence for highly misaligned firms, consistent with benchmarking as a stabilizing response.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/value: The study introduces a theory of strategic correctness into the convergencedivergence literature by distinguishing descriptive benchmark movement from alignmentconditioned strategic coherence. It repositions benchmarking as a contingent response that depends on internal-external alignment rather than as a dominant strategic logic.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Practical implications: The results provide a diagnostic framework for determining when benchmark-following is likely to stabilize misalignment and when internal coherence enables sustained strategic becoming.
</div>

This paper specifies the Generative Brand Mention Framework (GBMF), a proposed open measurement specification for brand representation in AI-generated responses. GBMF comprises three separable measures. Brand-mention visibility (MV) estimates the probability that the brand appears in an AI response to a category-relevant prompt. Brand-mention alignment (MA) measures, given a mention, the consistency of the description against the brand's own declared facts, not external truth. The "alignment" construct here concerns factual correspondence, distinct from the AI-alignment construct that concerns model behaviour. Brand-mention sentiment (MS) measures, given a mention, whether the response speaks positively or negatively about the brand. MA and MS each condition on mention.&nbsp;
<div>
 The three measures are necessary because the underlying mechanisms are separable. A brand can be mentioned yet described inaccurately, or described accurately yet positioned unfavourably, and these are different remediation problems.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 MV and MA are reported on a 0-100 scale; MS as three percentage shares plus a net figure on-100 to +100, over a governed prompt set and a declared engine set.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 GBMF specifies formal definitions, error quantification, companion statistics, evaluator architecture with a determinism-tested control arm, governance requirements, and pre-stated falsifiability criteria. An end-toend worked example accompanies the specification, with per-prompt per-engine mention rates published as supplementary data. The framework is published in full to enable open, governed, falsifiable measurement.
</div>

Purpose. This paper models how split payment, the segregation of IBS/CBS at financial settlement under Brazil's consumption-tax reform, affects corporate financial management and the cost of capital, recasting a managerial reading of the reform as an organizational shock into a formal, testable framework.&nbsp;
<div>
 Design / methodology / approach. The paper is conceptual. It adapts the Modigliani-Miller and Brusov-Filatova theory of the timing and frequency of tax payments, built for the profit tax, to indirect taxation, deriving a closed-form change in the weighted average cost of capital (ΔWACC), calibrated at a 26.5% benchmark, an official estimate rather than a fixed rate or ceiling.&nbsp;
 <div>
  Findings. Split payment is modelled as the limit of maximum-frequency, anticipated collection. As the indirect tax yields no shield, the relevant object is the float on collected tax, which segregation eliminates, raising the cost of capital. Variation is driven mainly by the interception base and refund lag. Scenarios yield increases of 0.36-0.93 percentage points, value losses near 6-7%, and a sector crossover by refund period.&nbsp;
 </div>
 <div>
  Research limitations / implications. The results are calibrated illustrations, not empirical estimates, and the homology with the profit-tax framework is structural but sign-reversed. The two interception designs are analytical bounding cases, not the statutory procedures themselves.&nbsp;
 </div>
 <div>
  Practical implications. For firms, shortening the refund lag and enrolling in compliance programmes are first-order levers; for policy, the reform's neutrality depends on reconciliation and credit reimbursement keeping pace with segregation. Originality / value. The paper offers the first transposition of the tax-timing cost-of-capital framework from direct to indirect taxation, introduces a float-based ΔWACC, and proposes a sector-design typology of the split-payment shock.
 </div>
</div>
